.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_set_header \- set a request header, or replace one
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_request_set_header(chttp_request_t *req,
                                       const char *name,
                                       const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function puts the header with the name that you give into the
request, or replaces it. The request stores each header name in
lowercase, so every later lookup ignores the case of the name. If a
header with the same name is already there (the match of the name
ignores the case), the function writes the new value over the old one.

The library makes the map of the headers on the first call.

name must be a token that is not empty and that holds only tchar bytes
of RFC 7230 SS3.2.6: every byte must be an ASCII letter, an ASCII
digit, or one of "!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~". A name of zero length has no valid
form on the wire. A name with any other byte in it, such as a space or
a literal ':', is not a way to inject a CRLF, but it still makes a line
on the wire with a broken structure, which a strict server or proxy
further down can read in the wrong way.

value must not hold a CR byte or an LF byte inside it. The serializer
writes "name: value\er\en" onto the wire with no escape, and a caller
can reflect untrusted data into a header, such as a token that it
forwards or a header from a proxy. Without this check, a CR or an LF in
value lets that caller inject any number of extra header lines, or cut
the request into two, which is HTTP request splitting. The function
therefore gives ccol_invalid_args for such a name or value, and stores
nothing.

The function also rejects a name of "Transfer-Encoding" (the match of
that name ignores the case). chttpclient never applies a transfer
coding to a request body: it always sends a request with a body whole,
and frames it with Content-Length. Without this rejection, the
automatic Content-Length of chttpclient_do puts a Content-Length
header beside the Transfer-Encoding header on the wire, while the body
carries no transfer coding. That framing is ambiguous, and the request
and response parser of this library rejects such a framing in a
message that it parses (RFC 7230 SS3.3.3).

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" header, because it has no
body length to check the value against, and a check here would also
need a copy of the method and of the redirect context of the request,
which come later. The client validates the header later, when the
request reaches the wire. On a request that carries a body (one that
uses the method POST, PUT or PATCH), chttpclient_do and every other
function that runs a request reject a bad Content-Length from the
caller. A good value is a plain unsigned string
of decimal digits, with no leading '+' or '-' and no whitespace
inside it, and it must also match the body that the client sends
exactly. The reason is the same as for rejecting Transfer-Encoding
above: the declared framing and the wire must not disagree. See
chttpclient_do(3).

A header that you set here follows every redirect, with two
exceptions. A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the original
request carries no Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2 or WWW-Authenticate
header that you set. A hop whose host differs carries no Host header
that you set, and the client writes its own. Each hop is compared with
the original request, so a hop back to the original origin carries
them again. A host compares without regard to case. See
chttpclient_do(3).

The client writes a default value for Host, Accept, User-Agent,
Content-Length, Content-Type, Authorization and Expect when it needs
one. When you set a header with one of these names here, the client
does not write the default, and sends your value exactly as you gave
it; this is also true for Host.

Only POST, PUT and PATCH have a body. For all other methods, the client
does not send a Content-Length, Content-Type or Expect header that you
set, because there is no body for the header to describe. This is also
true on a hop that a 301, 302 or 303 redirect changed into a GET.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req     The request to change. It must not be NULL.
name    The header name, for example "Authorization". It must not be
        NULL and must not be empty. It must hold only tchar bytes of
        RFC 7230. It must not be "Transfer-Encoding".
value   The header value. It must not be NULL. It must not hold a CR
        byte or an LF byte.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            The request has the header.
ccol_invalid_args       req, name or value is NULL; or name is empty,
                        or holds a byte outside the tchar set of RFC
                        7230; or value holds a CR byte or an LF byte
                        inside it; or name is "Transfer-Encoding".
ccol_not_enough_memory  The allocation of the map failed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_get_header (3)
